<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/009A">Warning <span data-tag="009A">2.2.6.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be bicategories which are strictly isomorphic. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is strict if and only if $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is strict. If we assume only that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ are isomorphic (rather than strictly isomorphic), then we cannot draw the same conclusion. In other words, the condition that a bicategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is strict is invariant under <em>strict</em> isomorphism, but not under isomorphism. </p>
